From: lineage-sc-06
Subject: [PATCH] nmi326: free the IRQ on release instead of leaking it

isdbt_release() resets irq_status and kfree()s pdev, but never calls
free_irq(). pdev is the dev_id the interrupt was requested with, so a process
that exits without sending IOCTL_ISDBT_INTERRUPT_UNREGISTER leaves the IRQ
registered against freed memory. The next interrupt runs the handler on a
freed pdev.

It also produces a confusing symptom later. A subsequent open gets a fresh
pdev, REGISTER/ENABLE/DISABLE walk the state machine normally, and then
UNREGISTER calls free_irq() with the new pdev - which does not match the
dev_id of the action still registered from the previous open. __free_irq()
walks the action list, finds no match, and warns:

  WARNING: at kernel/irq/manage.c:1183 __free_irq+0x88/0x1b8()
  Trying to free already-free IRQ 350
  [<c050f0cc>] (isdbt_ioctl+0x1e4/0x2fc) from ...

Observed on a stock SC-06D (4.0.4) while probing the tuner. Harmless in
itself, but it points at the use-after-free above.

Free the interrupt in release() when userspace did not. free_irq() can
sleep, so it runs outside the spinlock; the decision is taken under the lock
so a concurrent ioctl cannot race the state transition.

diff --git a/drivers/media/nmi326/nmi326.c b/drivers/media/nmi326/nmi326.c
index 5c93e0d..e53f450 100644
--- a/drivers/media/nmi326/nmi326.c
+++ b/drivers/media/nmi326/nmi326.c
@@ -127,11 +127,35 @@ static int isdbt_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
 static int isdbt_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
 {
 	struct ISDBT_OPEN_INFO_T *pdev;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int registered;
+
 	pdev = (struct ISDBT_OPEN_INFO_T *)(filp->private_data);
 
 	NM_KMSG("<isdbt> isdbt release\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * The interrupt was requested with pdev as its dev_id. Releasing the
+	 * file without freeing it leaves the IRQ pointing at memory this
+	 * function is about to kfree(), so the next interrupt would run the
+	 * handler against a freed pdev - and a later UNREGISTER from a new
+	 * open would call free_irq() with a dev_id the registered action does
+	 * not match, which is the "Trying to free already-free IRQ" warning.
+	 *
+	 * Free it here if userspace did not. free_irq() may sleep, so it has
+	 * to happen outside the spinlock; the flag is read under the lock so
+	 * a concurrent ioctl cannot race the state.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&pdev->isr_lock, flags);
+	registered = (pdev->irq_status != DTV_IRQ_DEINIT);
 	pdev->irq_status = DTV_IRQ_DEINIT;
 	g_bCatchIrq = 0;
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pdev->isr_lock, flags);
+
+	if (registered) {
+		NM_KMSG("<isdbt> release: freeing IRQ left registered\n");
+		free_irq(gpio_cfg.irq, pdev);
+	}
 
 	kfree(pdev->rwBuf);
 	kfree((void *)pdev);
